// Regression (BUG#64 FIXED, native backend). A Vec (or any collection) element
// that is an aggregate LARGER than 8 bytes used to come back corrupt from
// `.get()` → `Some(elem)` → `.unwrap()`:
//   - Vec[P8]  (an 8-byte struct)  -> was already correct
//   - Vec[Big] (16-byte struct)    -> was garbage (no crash)
//   - Vec[Vec[i32]] (24-byte Vec)  -> was a SEGFAULT
//
// Root cause (x86_selector.ax regalloc_is_16byte_cached): OP_INDEX had no
// dedicated case, so it fell into the catch-all `else`, whose BUG#56 exclusion
// only covered tagged sum/option/result (kinds 6/11/12) — NOT a plain
// struct/array (kinds 1/3/8) that happens to be exactly 16 bytes (Big: 4xi32).
// Such an element is held BY ADDRESS in this backend like every other aggregate,
// so it was wrongly classified 16-byte-INLINE. `Vec[Big].get(i) -> Big` then hit
// OP_RETURN's is_16byte branch, which splits what is actually an 8-byte ELEMENT
// ADDRESS into two register loads (RDX = [addr+8], RAX = [addr]); when the temp
// address vreg happened to allocate to RDX itself, the first load clobbered it
// before the second load read it — garbage or a null-pointer segfault a level up.
// Fix: a NEW `elif op == OP_INDEX` case applies the `type_is_aggregate` exclusion
// (matching the OP_DEREF/OP_COPY/OP_CALL guards already fixed for BUG#56/#60).
// Scoped to OP_INDEX only — the general catch-all (used by OP_GET_FIELD etc.) is
// untouched, because a struct EMBEDDED by value in another struct (e.g.
// `Outer.inner: P` where P is a 16-byte struct) relies on the OLD narrow
// classification for its call-boundary register-pair ABI convention; widening
// the catch-all itself regressed tstruct_abi.
import std.collections

struct Big:
    a: i32
    b: i32
    c: i32
    d: i32

fn main() -> i32:
    mut v = Vec[Big].new()
    v.push(Big(a: 10, b: 12, c: 20, d: 0))
    let r = v.get(0)
    if r.is_some():
        let p = r.unwrap()
        if p.a + p.b + p.c + p.d == 42:
            println("big-elem-ok")
        else:
            println("big-elem-wrong")
    else:
        println("miss-wrong")
    return 0
